1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,536
How does one system talk to card networks that each speak a different language?

2
00:00:04,586 --> 00:00:10,272
In Lesson 7, ecom proved who it was, and the card zone agreed to charge the card.

3
00:00:10,322 --> 00:00:16,152
In Lesson 2, the charge then crossed the card network to the buyer's bank, which said yes or no.

4
00:00:16,202 --> 00:00:22,440
This lesson opens the last step inside the card zone: how it speaks to every processing centre.

5
00:00:22,490 --> 00:00:24,912
Abroad, you carry a plug adapter.

6
00:00:24,962 --> 00:00:27,240
Every country's socket is different.

7
00:00:27,290 --> 00:00:29,880
Your laptop's plug never changes.

8
00:00:29,930 --> 00:00:31,848
You never rewire the laptop.

9
00:00:31,898 --> 00:00:33,720
You just pick the right adapter.

10
00:00:33,770 --> 00:00:35,592
Payments have the same problem.

11
00:00:35,642 --> 00:00:42,408
Behind every card network sits a card processing centre: the switchboard that carries a payment to the buyer's bank.

12
00:00:42,458 --> 00:00:44,496
Each centre has its own protocol.

13
00:00:44,546 --> 00:00:49,920
It has its own field names, its own long list of error codes, and its own way of saying yes.

14
00:00:49,970 --> 00:00:57,168
If the card zone spoke every centre's language itself, every new centre would mean changing the code that guards card numbers.

15
00:00:57,218 --> 00:01:00,096
That code should change as rarely as possible.

16
00:01:00,146 --> 00:01:02,760
So the card zone has one adapter per centre.

17
00:01:02,810 --> 00:01:05,736
An adapter is a small, separate service.

18
00:01:05,786 --> 00:01:08,832
On one side, it speaks the centre's language.

19
00:01:08,882 --> 00:01:13,512
On the other side, it speaks one plain language that the card zone understands.

20
00:01:13,562 --> 00:01:17,832
Inside the card zone, there is one plug shape for every centre.

21
00:01:17,882 --> 00:01:19,704
Charge a card.

22
00:01:19,754 --> 00:01:21,576
Give money back.

23
00:01:21,626 --> 00:01:23,448
Ask what happened to a payment.

24
00:01:23,498 --> 00:01:26,544
The questions are the same, whichever centre answers.

25
00:01:26,594 --> 00:01:30,072
The card zone never needs to know which centre is behind the plug.

26
00:01:30,122 --> 00:01:31,944
Then come the answers.

27
00:01:31,994 --> 00:01:35,352
Each centre says yes and no in hundreds of ways.

28
00:01:35,402 --> 00:01:41,376
The adapter turns every answer into one of three outcomes: approved, declined, or unknown.

29
00:01:41,426 --> 00:01:43,608
Approved means the money moved.

30
00:01:43,658 --> 00:01:47,232
Declined means the buyer's bank said no, and no money moved.

31
00:01:47,282 --> 00:01:49,656
Unknown is the honest third answer.

32
00:01:49,706 --> 00:01:51,528
A timeout is unknown.

33
00:01:51,578 --> 00:01:54,936
A broken or unreadable answer is unknown.

34
00:01:54,986 --> 00:01:59,088
Unknown is never declined, because the money may already have moved.

35
00:01:59,138 --> 00:02:03,696
Picture the buyer's bank saying yes, and the answer getting lost on the way back.

36
00:02:03,746 --> 00:02:08,952
If the system read that silence as no, the buyer would pay again, and pay twice.

37
00:02:09,002 --> 00:02:11,784
So every charge carries our own payment id.

38
00:02:11,834 --> 00:02:14,256
The centre keeps that id with the payment.

39
00:02:14,306 --> 00:02:18,408
Later, the card zone can ask the centre about exactly that payment.

40
00:02:18,458 --> 00:02:20,280
Asking never moves money.

41
00:02:20,330 --> 00:02:22,152
It only reads what happened.

42
00:02:22,202 --> 00:02:24,024
Now watch the product.

43
00:02:24,074 --> 00:02:26,640
The buyer has typed a card and pressed Pay.

44
00:02:26,690 --> 00:02:30,672
The payment page shows a spinner and the words "Taking the payment".

45
00:02:30,722 --> 00:02:35,808
A short line says the buyer's bank is being asked, and that this takes a few seconds.

46
00:02:35,858 --> 00:02:41,328
Behind that line, the card zone hands the charge to the plug, and the adapter talks to its centre.

47
00:02:41,378 --> 00:02:44,064
In the usual case, a yes comes back.

48
00:02:44,114 --> 00:02:48,144
The page turns to "Paid" and shows a reference number from the network.

49
00:02:48,194 --> 00:02:57,168
If the bank says no, the page shows "Payment declined" with a plain reason, such as "There isn't enough money on the card." Now the centre goes silent.

50
00:02:57,218 --> 00:02:59,232
The page does not say declined.

51
00:02:59,282 --> 00:03:03,984
It says "We're confirming your payment", and it tells the buyer not to pay again.

52
00:03:04,034 --> 00:03:09,600
The result follows within minutes, once the system has asked the centre about that one payment id.

53
00:03:09,650 --> 00:03:12,168
On the bank's side, the team gets an alert.

54
00:03:12,218 --> 00:03:16,608
It names the adapter that could not reach its centre in the last few minutes.

55
00:03:16,658 --> 00:03:20,352
It warns that charges through that adapter fail or stay unknown.

56
00:03:20,402 --> 00:03:22,224
What does this protect?

57
00:03:22,274 --> 00:03:28,056
Without adapters, one centre's odd error code could break the code that guards every card number.

58
00:03:28,106 --> 00:03:33,672
Without the unknown outcome, a lost yes becomes a no, and a buyer pays twice.

59
00:03:33,722 --> 00:03:37,272
The adapter protects the card zone from every centre's quirks.

60
00:03:37,322 --> 00:03:39,960
The unknown outcome protects the buyer's money.

61
00:03:40,010 --> 00:03:45,072
And when the bank joins a new network, it adds a new adapter, and nothing else changes.

62
00:03:45,122 --> 00:03:46,944
Back to the travel plug.

63
00:03:46,994 --> 00:03:50,688
A fourth country, a fourth socket, a fourth adapter.

64
00:03:50,738 --> 00:03:53,160
The laptop stays exactly the same.

65
00:03:53,210 --> 00:03:57,072
One adapter per socket, and silence is never no.

66
00:03:57,122 --> 00:04:00,528
Next: how the card zone keeps a card number safe.
